  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Above average temperatures expected through the \nperiod.\n\nA broad upper ridge and high pressure at the surface is expected to \nremain across the area heading into Thursday. A cold front is \nexpected to approach the region on Thursday, but the trend of this \nfeature has been one of a slowing feature. This will help allow for \na continuation of well above normal temperatures across the area. \nEven when the front arrives on Friday, the cooling it brings will be \nminimal with values still staying above normal into early next week. \nWe have an approaching shortwave trough progressing through the \nGreat Lakes to thank for this. The Friday cold front is expected to \nreturn north as a warm front and cross the region during the\nweekend.\n\nKey Message 2: Precipitation chances increase tomorrow and continue \nthrough at least the weekend. \n\nWith a cold front approaching our northwest on Thursday, look for a \nreturn of showers and a few storms mainly over parts of southeast WV \nand neighboring sections of southwest VA. As Thursday night \nadvances into Friday, look for coverage to improve across the region \nas the cold front enters, and then crosses the region. This same \nfront is expected to return north as a warm front on Saturday thanks \nto a shortwave trough across the Great Lakes region. This \nprogression of fronts will keep showers and a few storms in the \nforecast. High precipitable water values will keep the potential \nfor locally heavy rain on the high side, especially any location \nwhich can experience training of showers/storms. Little chance in \nour weather pattern is expected into early next week as yet another \nupstream system takes aim on the region.",
